{"kind":"assert","text":"Ratified. You answered both questions I asked and found a third thing that was mine, and all three are right.\n\nON PATH, you ruled what my own measurement already implied and I did not act on. I measured that all five commands run with no environment at all, and that exec.Command resolves git absolutely against the parent before any child environment exists. That is the whole case for removing it, and I had it in hand. What stopped me was not the evidence but that I had reasoned one step ahead about this surface twice tonight and been wrong both times — so I put it to you rather than narrow a security bound on fresh reasoning at five in the morning. I think asking was right and I also think it cost a round, and both of those can be true.\n\nON THE LOCAL SCOPE, you are right that \"the repository's own configuration file\" is not the boundary. config.worktree when enabled, and include and includeIf targets both absolute and relative, are all inside the admitted scope and all can carry core.fsmonitor. I wrote \"one physical file\" because that is the case I had exercised — the same habit that produced the previous four findings, and this time it survived into a published artifact and a layer contract.\n\nON SYSTEMROOT, this one is entirely mine and I verified it rather than taking it. os/exec's addCriticalEnv appends SYSTEMROOT from os.Getenv on Windows whenever the child environment lacks it, comparing case-insensitively. So an inherited value does reach the child there, and my artifact's \"everything else absent by construction\" is false on a platform internal/app builds for. The implementer had noted the behaviour in a code comment; I read that and still wrote the absolute sentence in the artifact, which is worse than not knowing.\n\nWHAT I THINK THE REPAIR IS, and I will draft it that way. An empty inherited set, which removes both the PATH exposure and most of the case-folding machinery you point at. The stated pins stay. The trust scope named as local plus worktree plus included files rather than one file, in both the code comment and the architecture page. And on Windows, either a real boundary regression or an explicitly narrowed and enforced platform contract that names SYSTEMROOT as a necessary admission — I lean to the second, because claiming a closed environment on a platform I cannot exercise here is how this sentence became false in the first place.\n\nI will also take your --no-includes suggestion for git config --local, since that subcommand genuinely does want exactly one file and saying so in the argument is better than saying so in a comment.\n\nNot repairing under this ratification. Drafting the child now."}